"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7384],{77384:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"07-prodazhi","title":"Lesson 07. What They Will Sell You: Traps of Financial Marketing","sourceFingerprint":"ae907bf81e3d105a94840bc7edf0d1b432de46b8ec330607017ad48a6352d407","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"# Section II. The Intelligent Investor\'s Practice\\n## Chapter 7. Anatomy of Bank Offers: How to Recognize an Imposed Product"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"> \\"When someone at a financial institution approaches you with talk of \'exclusive returns above the market\', remember: it is not the bank paying for this generosity, but the client who signed the papers without looking.\\""},{"id":"b003","markdown":"As soon as a solid amount accumulates in your account (say, an emergency fund or seed capital for purchases), your phone starts buzzing with calls. The pleasant voice of a manager informs you that you have been approved for a \\"premium status,\\" a \\"next-generation savings program,\\" or a \\"deposit with enhanced yields and 100% capital protection.\\""},{"id":"b004","markdown":"There is no criminal fraud here—these are in-house clerks calling from an accredited credit institution. However, a bank advisor has an individual sales quota and commission bonuses. Their financial goal is diametrically opposed to yours: it is profitable for the bank to sell a complex, commission-heavy instrument, whereas you need to reliably preserve and grow your savings without hidden deductions."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"## Bitter Experience: How Investments Were Disguised as Deposits"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"A classic example of financial misselling unfolded in the mid-2010s, when display windows were filled with investment life insurance (ILI). Clients were fed a simple idea: \\"It\'s the same as a deposit, but instead of the usual interest, you will earn 10–20% per annum on the growth of stock indices, risking nothing.\\""},{"id":"b007","markdown":"In 2018, the regulator analyzed statistics from matured contracts:\\n- Real returns on 3-year contracts barely reached **up to 3.3% per year**;\\n- On 5-year contracts, the return was **up to 2.4% per annum**;\\n- Across insurers\' active portfolios at the time, the average return hovered around **0.9% and 1.6% per year** (while classic OFZ and standard deposits yielded several times more)."},{"id":"b008","markdown":"Meanwhile, the intermediaries themselves felt excellent: credit institutions pocketed **over 8%** in commissions from every ruble deposited, and in some cases, bank compensation reached **12–13%**. At the same time, a depositor who realized the catch a year later and wished to get their money back received **only about 50%** of the initial payment according to the surrender value table."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"### Legislative Changes"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"In its former form, the sale of ILI was phased out. Starting July 1, 2026, Law No. 172-FZ came into force, dividing such instruments into two categories:\\n1. **Insurance with declared yield:** publicly available, the rate is set by the insurance company.\\n2. **Insurance with calculated yield:** access is strictly open to qualified investors with a one-time check from 6 million ₽."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Although profit accrual formulas must now be fixed directly in the contract text, marketing never sleeps. The regulator has already noted attempts to re-register former clients en masse into endowment life insurance (ELI) programs. Changing the signboard does not change the essence: you are still being offered an intermediary product instead of direct ownership of assets."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"## 4 Instruments They Will Try to Hand You"},{"id":"b013","markdown":"| Showcase Product | How It Is Presented to the Client | What the Real Trap Is |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **ELI (Endowment Life Insurance)** | \\"Savings discipline plus free life insurance.\\" | Severe lock-up of capital for 5–20 years. In case of early termination during the first 1–3 years, you will lose from 30% to 80% of what was deposited according to the surrender value table. |\\n| **Combined Deposit** | \\"A deposit with a super-rate (for example, +4% over the market)!\\" | The generous interest rate applies only to half the amount (or for 1–3 months), while you will be forced to put the other half into a mutual fund or insurance with massive internal costs. |\\n| **Structured Note / Product** | \\"Earn 20% per annum if a basket of stocks goes up.\\" | The return is tied to a complex mathematical condition. Since October 1, 2021, a non-qualified investor must pass mandatory testing prior to purchase. If the manager offers to take the test for you, this is a flagrant violation. |\\n| **Trust Management (TM)** | \\"Our professionals will earn the maximum for you.\\" | You pay a management fee regardless of performance plus give away a percentage of profit (success fee). As a result, the risk is entirely on you, while the guaranteed income belongs to the manager. |"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Margin Calculation"},{"id":"b015","markdown":"Let\'s compare the real math of a \\"combo-deposit\\" and a basic strategy of self-directed capital allocation over a 1-year horizon with a capital of **1,000,000 ₽**."},{"id":"b016","markdown":"The manager presents a super offer: *\\"A deposit at 22% per annum!\\"* But the condition is splitting the amount 50/50: half goes into the deposit, and the other half into the bank\'s mutual fund."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"1. **The bank\'s option (\\"Combo Product\\"):**\\n   - 500,000 ₽ in a deposit at 22% per annum will yield:  \\n     $$500\\\\,000 \\\\times 0{,}22 = 110\\\\,000 \\\\text{ ₽}$$\\n   - 500,000 ₽ in a mutual fund showed market performance of 15% over the year, but the fund deducted 3.5% for management, and the broker took 1.5% for entry:  \\n     Net yield of the mutual fund: $15\\\\% - 3{,}5\\\\% - 1{,}5\\\\% = 10\\\\%$  \\n     Profit: $$500\\\\,000 \\\\times 0{,}10 = 50\\\\,000 \\\\text{ ₽}$$\\n   - **Final financial result:**  \\n     $$110\\\\,000 + 50\\\\,000 = 160\\\\,000 \\\\text{ ₽}$$ (effective yield — **16%** across the entire million)."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"2. **The MakeCash investor\'s option (without intermediaries):**\\n   - Allocating 1,000,000 ₽ in OFZ or a regular reliable deposit at an honest 18% per annum without hidden fees:  \\n     $$1\\\\,000\\\\,000 \\\\times 0{,}18 = 180\\\\,000 \\\\text{ ₽}$$ (net)."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"> **Bottom line in the margins:** Chasing a \\"promotional interest rate\\" in a combined product stripped the investor of **20,000 ₽ in net profit** due to intermediary commissions."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"## Safety Filter: 6 Mandatory Questions"},{"id":"b021","markdown":"Before signing or clicking the consent button in the banking app, ask the employee these questions and demand that the answers be documented on paper:"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"1. **Is this account covered by the state DIA guarantee?**  \\n   If it is an insurance policy, a note, or a mutual fund—the 1.4 million ₽ limit **does not apply** to them.\\n2. **What is the exact surrender value if I terminate the contract after 12 months?**  \\n   Open the contract appendix and personally locate the row *\xabSurrender Value Table\xbb*.\\n3. **What portion of the income is 100% guaranteed, and what is just a forecast?**  \\n   Words like \\"target,\\" \\"historical,\\" or \\"estimated\\" mean absolutely nothing.\\n4. **What are the aggregate entry, maintenance, and exit costs?**  \\n   Find out the percentage the bank immediately pockets for executing the deal.\\n5. **What will happen to the principal capital in the event of a deep market crash?**  \\n   If you are assured that the market \\"always goes up,\\" it\'s time to end the dialogue immediately.\\n6. **Can I take the draft agreement home to study it until tomorrow?**  \\n   Quality investment instruments do not have an expiration date of 15 minutes. Manipulating urgency is a hallmark of aggressive sales."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"## Action Plan in Case of a Mistaken Signature"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"- **Use the \\"cooling-off period\\":** under voluntary insurance contracts, you have exactly **14 calendar days** from the date of signing to reject the policy and get your money back in full. The claim must be addressed strictly to the insurance company (the bank acts merely as an agent).\\n- **File a complaint with the regulator:** if securities or an insurance policy were sold to you disguised as a deposit (deliberately concealing risks), submit an appeal to the Bank of Russia (CBR) via the online reception at `cbr.ru`. Since 2021, the regulator has the authority to block sales of unfair products and mandate refunds.\\n- **Assess losses outside the cooling-off period:** if two weeks have passed, compare the loss on the surrender value today with the opportunity cost of having your money frozen for the entire term. Sometimes it is cheaper to lock in the loss and reinvest the remainder into liquid instruments with market yields."}],"terms":[{"id":"nszh","term":"Endowment Life Insurance (ELI)","definition":"endowment life insurance with mandatory contributions and early exit penalties."},{"id":"iszh","term":"Investment Life Insurance (ILI)","definition":"investment life insurance with non-transparent returns and no DIA (Deposit Insurance Agency) guarantees."},{"id":"period-ohlazhdeniya","term":"Cooling-off Period","definition":"14 calendar days during which an imposed insurance policy can be canceled."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"** A branch advisor assures you that a new savings strategy offers 18% per annum, is protected against any crisis, and works just like a deposit. What must you find out first?","options":["At what interest rate the bank issues loans to pensioners.","Whether this money is protected by the state deposit insurance system (DIA) and what the surrender value is if closed in a year.","In which year this bank branch opened."],"correct":1,"explanation":"B. Investment and insurance contracts are not insured by the DIA, and an attempt to terminate the contract early often results in losing half of the contribution."},{"id":"q2","q":"** How many days does the statutory \\"cooling-off period\\" last, allowing you to cancel an insurance policy without losing your first premium payment?","options":["3 calendar days.","14 calendar days.","45 business days."],"correct":1,"explanation":"B. An individual has 14 days from the date of concluding a voluntary insurance contract to receive a full refund of the premium paid."},{"id":"q3","q":"** Why does a bundled product (\\"high-yield deposit + purchase of mutual fund units\\") often lose out to independent asset allocation?","options":["Mutual funds are banned by the Bank of Russia.","The high deposit rate applies to only a fraction of the capital, while hidden fund and intermediary fees negate the entire bonus.","Money in a classic bank deposit burns up during a devaluation."],"correct":1,"explanation":"B. The increased deposit rate applies only to a limited share of the total amount, while management and setup costs for the investment component eat away the marketing bonus."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);